td.paragraph:
Infants: 25 units/kg/hour to 30 units/kg/hour (continuous intravenous infusion);
Infants less than 2 months have the highest requirements (average 28 units/kg/hour)
Children greater than 1 year of age: 18 units/kg/hour to 20 units/kg/hour (continuous intravenous infusion);
Older children may require less heparin, similar to weight-adjusted adult dosage.

text.paragraph:
Hemorrhage is the chief complication that may result from heparin therapy [see 

                                    Warnings and Precautions (5.2)

                                 ]. An overly prolonged clotting time or minor bleeding during therapy can usually be controlled by withdrawing the drug [see Overdosage (10)]. Gastrointestinal or urinary tract bleeding during anticoagulant therapy may indicate the presence of an underlying occult lesion. Bleeding can occur at any site but certain specific hemorrhagic complications may be difficult to detect:
